egistry data, I see that many of the in_progress experiments already have substantial results collected, so the truly outstanding work is concentrated in the planned experiments. I should generate predictions for both categories since they both represent work that still needs completion or finalization. Now I'm structuring the YAML output to capture these predictions...\n\nFor the top tier, I'm examining the VTR results on SmolVLA where I already have Spatial and Goal performance but need the Long suite data to complete the picture. I'm also cross-referencing the π0.5 baseline results in the registry—there's already extensive data showing strong success rates across the object, spatial, and goal suites, with the long suite at 92%.\n\n8%, plus substantial pruning results that essentially make this one done. For OFT, the previous evaluation hit hardware constraints with Blackwell GPUs, so the best outcome would be finding compatible hardware to match the OpenVLA pattern. The Perceiver Resampler work for RoboFlamingo had training issues before, but successful completion with distinct pruning patterns would be ideal. Among the in-progress experiments, the learned reencoding on SmolVLA is nearly finished pending remaining seeds, the VTR universality test on OpenVLA already shows M=128 at 0%, and the XVLA threshold work is already complete with k=0.8 and k=0.85 data collected.\n\nI still need CV measurements for the diagnostic OFT work, and the LIBERO suite expansion has generated massive amounts of data. The OpenVLA M-sweep training attempts have been failing or getting killed. Let me work through predictions for all the incomplete experiments under the best-case scenario with detailed rationales for what each would show.\n\nFor the LIBERO suite VTR validation on SmolVLA, I already have M=32 results for Spatial at 53.2% and Goal at 56.4%, so I'm predicting M=32 Long would fall in the 50-55% range with consistent recovery from the initial 0% baseline.\n\nThe π0.5 immune confirmation experiment is actually mostly done based on the registry with tons of data already collected—under best conditions, the remaining work would just confirm cross-suite consistency with CV around 0.055 and show that the L2norm plateau and random cliff behavior generalizes across Spatial and Goal tasks.\n\nFor the OFT graded degradation work, I need compatible hardware or a workaround, and the best case would complete the CV measurements.\n\nThe OpenVLA results confirm the same graded pattern with CV≈0.219, and I'm expecting success rates around 35-40% at k=0.5, matching what OpenVLA shows.\n\nFor the Perceiver Resampler fine-tuning with pruning, previous training attempts failed, but the best outcome would be successful training with moderate baseline success rates—the Perceiver's cross-attention mechanism is similar to token-entangling but with controlled reduction, so I'm expecting intermediate behavior, possibly threshold-like.\n\nThe learned re-encoding work on SmoLVLA is in progress and just needs tri-seed completion for all M values and evaluations on the remaining seeds—I already have extensive 3-seed data, and the best case would confirm the flat M-curve holds across all seeds with tight confidence intervals.\n\nFor the method universality test on OpenVLA, M=128 failed completely at 0% success rate, so I'm hoping VTC-only at M=32 or M=64 shows some recovery, or a clean failure that strengthens the narrative around connector dependency—given the data, continued failure is actually useful for the paper.\n\nThe xVLA threshold localization work is complete with k=0.8 at ~89% and k=0.85 at ~68%, all confirmed across three seeds.\n\nI still need to measure the OFT coefficient of variation to complete the diagnostic table, ideally getting CV≈0.219 to match the pattern.\n\nThe LIBERO suite expansion is underway with cross-suite data already accumulated.\n\nFor the OpenVLA M-sweep with VTC, training attempts have failed so far, but I'm looking for at least one M value that shows partial recovery or a clean failure pattern.\n\nNow I'm checking whether the axis-2 interaction holds for π0.5 Goal/Long tasks the same way it does for Object tasks—I have the random pruning data showing a cliff pattern, and I need to verify the difference between entangling and independent patterns is at least 20 percentage points. Looking at the numbers, π0.5 with independent connector shows Object at k=0.9 random dropping to 9% versus Goal at k=0.9 random at 62.2% compared to its 96% baseline.\n\nActually, all these drops are dramatic, which suggests π0.5 behaves uniformly critical rather than truly independent like I expected. Comparing to X-VLA's entangling connector, the pattern is clearer—Object barely drops from 99.2% to 97.6%, but Goal falls 27.8 percentage points from 98% to 70.2%, and Long crashes from 94% to 47%. So the axis-2 amplification effect is really about entangling connectors being far more sensitive to task complexity than independent ones.\n\nNow I'm looking at the specific amplification numbers: X-VLA shows a 27.4 percentage point gap between Object and Goal performance at k=0.9, which is the core finding. For π0.5 with L2norm, the pattern should follow the same method and k-value comparison, but I need to check where the Object performance lands to see if the amplification story holds.\n\nLooking at the L2norm results across both models, π0.5 shows much smaller drops when moving from Object to other tasks—only 7.6pp to Goal and 17.2pp to Long—whereas X-VLA's amplification is dramatically larger at 27.4pp to Goal and even steeper at higher k values. This confirms that entanglement in X-VLA creates substantially more amplification than the independent π0.5 model.\n\nNow I'm drafting the full YAML configuration and need to identify which experiments are truly planned versus in progress. Looking at the experiment plan, the core planned experiments are the four main ones plus several in-progress experiments that still have remaining work to complete.\n\nLet me reconsider what the user is asking for—they want predictions for each non-completed experiment in the plan, so I should include both the explicitly planned and in-progress ones. Now I'm thinking through the predicted results for each experiment under the best-tier assumptions, where entangling connectors show at least 20 percentage points of amplification and independent connectors show at most 10 percentage points.\n\nFor the attention-based pruning experiments on X-VLA, I'm looking at the existing evaluation results to establish baselines. The last-layer attention mechanism is performing strongest on object-centric tasks at around 99%, while mean attention pooling drops slightly to 98%, and rollout attention is at 97%. For the longer-horizon tasks, the last-layer approach maintains 90% success rate, though the data cuts off before I can see the complete picture.\n\nNow I need to identify which experiments are still in the planning phase versus actively running to understand what gaps remain to fill. I'm looking at the metric ranges for the predictions and need to be careful about what values are realistic for each experiment. Now I'm thinking through how to interpret the user's request — whether \"planned\" refers strictly to experiments with that status in the YAML or more broadly to all experiments in the plan. I should construct the YAML output with specific numerical predictions for each planned experiment, including the expected results, rationale, and analysis sections. Looking at the data I already have, most of the c1_pi05_immune_confirmation predictions are essentially confirmed—the random performance floors and generalization patterns match what's already been measured. The experiments that actually need fresh predictions are the ones still in planned status: the OFT graded full run, the Perceiver Resampler training for the third connector, and a few others downstream.\n\nFor c6_libero_suite_expansion, the bulk of data collection is done, but I still need to run some cross-suite evaluations with OpenVLA and SmolVLA to fill in the remaining gaps. Now I'm going through the full experiment list systematically to generate predictions for each one that's still pending. Looking at the active and planned experiments, I need to generate predictions for the YAML output, focusing on the ones that aren't complete or abandoned. I'm thinking through how to structure the best tier predictions for each of these ongoing and planned experiments.\n\nFor the learned reencoding work with SmolVLA, I'm expecting the M-curve to stay flat across all seeds with overlapping confidence intervals, and the VTR recovery ratio should land near-lossless around 0.95-1.05 once those final seed confirmations finish. On the universality front with OpenVLA, the VTC-only M=32 training should show partial recovery of about 30-40% compared to the hard pruning baseline of 0%, though it'll still fall short of the full OpenVLA baseline at 74%, which suggests the connector approach works much better for pixel-shuffle than for MLP layers.\n\nThe M-sweep across M=32/64/96 will either show a clean failure pattern that isolates VTR to pixel-shuffle specifically, or a partial recovery that's clearly weaker than what SmolVLA achieved. For the threshold localization work, I already have the sharp transition confirmed across three seeds with minimal standard deviation, so that's essentially complete. The OFT CV measurement is also done—the registry already has the value at 0.219. The Libero suite expansion is still in heavy data collection mode.\n\nOnce all the remaining cells in the model×suite×method matrix are filled, I should see the two-axis framework validated with entangling connectors showing amplified effects under more complex tasks. For the VTR work on Spatial/Goal/Long, I have the M=32 results for Spatial and Goal already, just need the Long variant which should show recovery from that zero percent cliff, potentially with multiple M values to trace the full curve. The π0.5 immune confirmation across all four suites has extensive data available, and under the best case scenario it should uniformly confirm the critical behavior with the L2norm plateau holding steady at k≥0.85.\n\nFor OFT with pruning, I'm looking for compatible hardware to run the full baseline plus pruning curves across all suites, expecting Object detection around 97% to match official numbers and smooth degradation patterns like OpenVLA, with the CV metric suggesting the connector rather than the action head drives the behavior. The Perceiver Resampler experiment on LIBERO should show successful fine-tuning with reasonable baseline performance, where the learnable cross-attention queries create intermediate entanglement, and pruning curves should reveal distinct patterns that differentiate this connector type from the others.\n\nNow I'm working through the YAML configuration with careful attention to the amplification metrics—specifically calculating the performance gap between X-VLA Object and Goal conditions at k=0.9, where the 27.4 percentage point difference falls within the target range, though I need to verify the k=0.95 cases more precisely. 7.6pp ≤10pp ✓, and at k=0.85 it hits 10pp which is borderline but still within bounds. So the independent connector pattern holds up under the best tier conditions.\n\nNow looking at the cross-validation strategy results, I'm seeing a clear monotonic relationship between coefficient of variation and performance change across the three architectures—π0.5 shows the largest gains around 36pp, X-VLA sits in the middle at 27pp, and OpenVLA actually declines slightly. The Spearman correlation between absolute performance delta and CV magnitude is perfect, indicating a clean rank ordering.\n\nFor the cross-suite framework accuracy metric, I'm checking how performance scales across the 90-100 range.\n\nMoving into the remaining predictions, I need to verify the VTR recovery ratio stays within 0.95-1.05 bounds—the M=128 passthrough at 58% versus the 59.2% baseline gives me 0.98, which is solid. The π0.5 L2norm gap between the k=0.5 configuration and random baseline is 36pp, right in the target 30-40pp window. Now I'm working through the specific experiment predictions, starting with what SmolVLA M=32 would achieve on the Long variant of the LIBERO suite under VTR conditions.\n\nFor the VTR Long setup, SmolVLA M=32 should recover to roughly 33-37% performance, mirroring the near-lossless recovery pattern I've seen in Object, Spatial, and Goal tasks. The OFT Object baseline sits around 97%, with L2norm pruning at k=0.5 dropping it to 37-40% and k=0.9 maintaining 62-65%, consistent with OpenVLA's degradation curve. I still need to estimate the OFT baselines for Spatial, Goal, and Long variants from the official results. For the RoboFlamingo connector, the Perceiver Resampler operates as a cross-attention mechanism using learnable query tokens to bridge visual and language representations.\n\nNow I'm structuring the full YAML configuration and cross-checking the E5 experiments — the registry shows strong E5 attention pruning results on Goal tasks (98.2% and 97.4% success rates), which supports the assumption that this method generalizes well across the suite.\n\nSince these E5 experiments are already completed but missing from the experiment plan, I'll note them as part of the narrative rather than as planned work. For the remaining 10 experiments with planned or in-progress status, I'll predict specific values based on what data is still needed and the patterns I'm seeing across the existing results.\n\nStarting with c4_learned_reencoding_smolvla—this one's essentially done since all the M-value evaluations are confirmed across three seeds, showing a flat compression curve with minimal loss. I'll predict M=16 at 56.9±1.3% and M=32 at 60.1% based on the measurements already in hand.\n\nFor c4_ext_method_universality_openvla, the VTR approach on OpenVLA is failing completely with M=128, which suggests the method might be specific to pixel-shuffle operations. I'm trying VTC-only training now to see if that succeeds or fails—if it works even partially, that's a positive signal for the method's broader applicability, but if it also fails, that's a clean negative result showing VTR really is pixel-shuffle-dependent.\n\nLooking at the paper's narrative, we've already established that VTR doesn't help MLP projectors, so the framing is really about how connector type determines whether VTR helps. The cleanest outcome here is if VTC-only also fails, which would reinforce that VTR is specifically needed for entangled connectors rather than being a general improvement.\n\nNow moving to the OpenVLA m-sweep experiments with M=32/64/96 using VTC training. Failed or killed. Under best conditions, training completes with evaluation showing that M=96 and M=128 both hit 0% success rate, M=64 also reaches 0%, while M=32 shows a slight 0-5% improvement—suggesting VTR doesn't meaningfully help MLP projectors regardless of model size.\n\nFor the threshold localization work, I've already got the 3-seed results: k=0.80 yields 89.0±0.72pp and k=0.85 gives 67.9±0.31pp, showing a non-monotonic drop of 21.1pp between them. The diagnostic OFT coefficient of variation is confirmed at 0.219 from the registry analysis.\n\nNow looking at the LIBERO suite expansion—there's already substantial data collected, and I'm predicting the remaining OpenVLA cross-suite results with L2norm and random thresholds, plus filling in the Goal and Long baseline gaps where OpenVLA Spatial L2norm at k=0.9 should land around 75% and Goal L2norm at the same threshold around 74.8%. VLA Long baseline: 52.8% (done ✓)\n\nSmolVLA cross-suite (all pruning = 0%):\n- SmolVLA Spatial k=0.95: ~4-7% (random=4.2%, L2norm=6.6% from registry ✓)\n- SmolVLA Goal k=0.95: ~3-4% (random=4.2%, L2norm=3.4% ✓)\n- SmolVLA Long k=0.95: 0% (done ✓)\n\nπ0.5 cross-suite — extensive data exists:\n- All L2norm plateau confirmed\n- All random cliff confirmed\n\nX-VLA cross-suite is nearly complete with Spatial, Goal, and Long data collected. X-VLA Long k=0.5 random came in at 61.6% and L2norm at 44.6%, filling the remaining gaps and validating the 2-axis framework without exceptions.\n\nFor the VTR experiment on the Long suite, I'm expecting SmolVLA with M=32 to recover around 33-38% performance, staying near-lossless compared to the 36.6% baseline, with the M-curve remaining flat across both M=32 and M=64 settings. The π0.5 immune confirmation already has extensive baseline data showing Object at 98.2% and Spatial at 97.2%.\n\nThe remaining work is synthesis and confirmation—no new experiments are needed. The key finding emerging is that at extreme compression on Long, L2norm slightly outperforms random (50.2% vs 46.8%, a +3.4pp gap), which reverses the pattern seen on Object and Spatial and aligns with the k=0.3 reversal behavior.\n\nFor the OFT evaluation, I need compatible hardware to test whether the scaling law holds across different architectures—expecting OFT Object baseline around 97% and L2norm compression at k=0.5 to drop to roughly 37-40%.\n\nFor RoboFlamingo with the Perceiver Resampler, I'm looking at a moderate baseline of 55-65% on LIBERO given it's a first fine-tuning attempt, with the cross-attention mechanism creating intermediate dependencies that should produce graded degradation under pruning.\n\nI'm reconsidering whether to include this as a third connector family experiment since the paper already covers five models across three families—adding a fourth might be nice but isn't critical, so I'll plan for it to either succeed and provide an additional data point or be noted as future work. Now I need to structure the YAML carefully with specific numbers, and I'm also wondering whether to incorporate the E5 attention pruning experiments from the registry that weren't explicitly in the original plan. Writing the YAML structure... Now I'm realizing the key distinction—the pruning tolerance is determined by the connector architecture itself, not the baseline performance. Since OFT uses the same connector as OpenVLA, the relative degradation pattern should mirror OpenVLA's, but because OFT has a higher baseline (97% vs 74%), the absolute success rates at each k value should be proportionally higher. Let me recalculate by looking at the actual OpenVLA degradation curve more carefully to apply that same relative drop to OFT's stronger starting point. Now I'm examining the Perceiver Resampler architecture in the c5_third_connector_roboflamingo model—it uses 64 learned query tokens that attend to all 256 vision patches through cross-attention, creating a natural compression where each output token depends on the full input. This is quite different from simpler approaches like linear projections or pixel-shuffling, so pruning output tokens could be particularly damaging since removing any single token loses information aggregated from many patches, though the learned query structure might distribute information more gracefully than pixel-level approaches.\n\nFor the best tier predictions, I'm estimating a moderate coefficient of variation around 0.15-0.25 depending on how specialized the queries become, with baseline performance on LIBERO Object around 50-65%, then dropping to 40-50% when removing 6-7 tokens at k=0.9, and further degrading to 15-30% at k=0.5—showing a graded-to-threshold pattern closer to X-VLA than SmolVLA. Given the infrastructure challenges from previous training attempts, the most realistic outcome is that training succeeds after fixing those issues, yielding moderate baseline success rates and confirming this architecture's distinct position in the pruning framework.\n\nNow I'm organizing the YAML output and realizing I need to include all experiments marked as planned or in-progress from the existing plan file. For the mostly complete experiments like the threshold localization work, I'll note their status and summarize the expected conclusions. I'm also mapping out the key tables and figures needed—a 2-axis framework table across models and suites, CV scaling laws, VTR M-curve results, and attention pruning comparisons, along with figures showing the framework overview, pruning curves per model, and cross-suite interactions.\n\nI'm now considering whether to include an E5 attention pruning experiment prediction in the best tier since it's framed as adding practical value to the paper, even though it's not formally in the existing plan. Looking at the paper structure, E5 pruning appears to be positioned as either a main section or supplementary contribution, so I should include it with predicted results for the object and long-context tasks.\n\nSince the E5 Goal suite data is already collected with strong performance (96.8-98.2%), I can confirm this assumption holds. There's still some remaining validation work around attention pruning on the π0.5 variant and potentially additional k values to explore.\n\nNow I'm organizing the YAML structure by experiment, with each entry containing the name, plan ID, description, and expected results metrics. I need to be careful with YAML formatting, especially ensuring all strings in tables use proper quoting.\n\nWriting the YAML now... Looking at the remaining experimental results across the different task categories—I can see the Random baseline for Object is nearly complete, while Spatial has a missing Random k=0.9 result that was killed, and Goal has most conditions filled in. For Long-horizon tasks, I need to check what baseline and variant results are still needed to complete the matrix. Now I'm looking at the X-VLA results across the remaining task categories—Spatial is wrapping up with strong performance across most k values, Goal shows interesting variability with L2norm and random performing similarly, and Long demonstrates the expected degradation pattern with lower scores overall, particularly at k=0.9 for L2norm.\n\nI've got comprehensive coverage across SmolVLA and π0.5 with all pruning methods showing near-zero impact, which is reassuring. The main gaps I'm tracking are OpenVLA's Spatial and Long performance under random pruning, plus some L2norm and attention-based pruning results that should fill in soon. Once those come through, I'll have the full picture to write out the YAML with all the experiment details organized by plan ID. Writing YAML output... Generating performance metrics table...\n\nLooking at OpenVLA's results, the amplification doesn't show the expected boost because its baseline performance is already quite strong across tasks—the Object detection at 70.6% compared to Goal at 74.8% suggests the model isn't benefiting from the connector in the same way. The real amplification story seems to emerge with the entangled connectors like X-VLA.\n\nNow I'll write out the YAML configuration to document these findings properly.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":149,"cache_read_input_tokens":130114,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":149},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"71bd17d0-2db3-4573-a2d4-98d47eafc079","uuid":"ab4e2e55-af5c-488c-bde3-40ff2e4ac965"}
